# Why Loom

The argument page answers the question a visitor brings once the landing has shown them what Loom is: why write a program this way at all. It opens on the question itself and a short answer, with two ways to join in beside it, drawn by the page head the other pages share. Then it makes the case in two forms. A question and its answer set out the motivation, and eight cards, in three groups, pair a problem in today's practice with what Loom does about it.

Nothing on the page answers a click with a message. Its links leave for GitHub or Telegram, and the nav, the pager and the footer around it belong to the page wrapper. So the page builds with Foldkit's inert builder, `ih`, and the view calls it without lending its own.

## The page head

The page opens on the head the other pages past the landing share: the Loom mark beside the question, a short answer under it, and the two cards that lead to the repository and the community group.

=>

const whyHead = (): Html =>
  pageHead(
    'why-top',
    'Why Loom?',
    "A program's reasoning belongs in the program. Literate programming has worked that way since 1984. Now that we write code with AI, it matters more than ever.",
  )

## The motivation

The case opens in prose, set as a problem and its answer side by side. The left column asks four questions a developer meets: a codebase they cannot find their way into, intent they have forgotten, documentation they cannot trust, and context that does not pass from one agent to the next. Then it names what the four share. Code records what a system does but not why, and the why drifts away into documents, tickets and memory.

The right column answers, and builds as it goes. It opens on where Loom keeps the why: in the program, as prose equal to the code it defines. Then it gives two reasons that matters. The first is people, who can come back to the codebase at any time and find its reasons written down. The second, and the strongest, is an agent, which reads the prose beside the code it changes and works from the intent instead of guessing at it.

=>

type Motivation = { readonly question: ReadonlyArray<string>; readonly answer: ReadonlyArray<string> }

const motivation: Motivation = {
  question: [
    "How do we find our way into a large codebase when we don't know where to start? How do we keep the intent behind code once it has left our memory? How do we know the documentation still describes the code? How do we carry context from one agent to the next, or from one model to another?",
    "Each of the questions asks the same thing: where does the meaning of the code live? Code records what a system does, but not why. The why ends up in documents, tickets and people's heads, and drifts away from the code it was meant to explain.",
  ],
  answer: [
    'In Loom, the why lives in the program. Prose and code are equal parts of one source. The prose defines what the system does and why, and the code does it.',
    'So the codebase stays open to people. We can return to it at any time, on our first day or years later, to review its quality, recover the intent behind a decision, audit its business logic, or plan the next change. No one has to reconstruct the reasons.',
    'With AI, this matters most of all. An agent reads the prose alongside the code it changes, so it works from our intent instead of guessing at it.',
  ],
}

## Problem and solution, in pairs

The rest of the case is made in pairs. Each card names a problem in the way software is written today, then what Loom does about it. A crimson bug marks the problem and a green check marks the answer, so the two lines need no legend.

The cards fall into three groups, by the kind of problem they answer. The first keeps prose and code in agreement. The second makes a program something a person can read from a beginning. The third is the tooling, which decides whether a team can take Loom up at all. One section head stands over all three, a woven emblem beside an eyebrow and a heading, the head every section of the site wears. Under it each group opens on a short title of its own, so a reader scanning the page sees the argument's three parts before reading any card.

Each card has a hue of its own, taken in turn from the mark's palette by `hueAt`, which wraps round when the cards outnumber the hues. The count runs on across the groups, so no two neighbouring groups open on the same hue. The hue stays hidden until the pointer reaches the card, then tints its ground and its edge, so a reader scanning the pairs can tell where they are.

=>

type Pair = { readonly problem: string; readonly answer: string }

type PairGroup = { readonly title: string; readonly pairs: ReadonlyArray<Pair> }

~

The emblem over the pairs draws a check on the site's navy ground. Its short stroke is the orange of a problem, and it turns at a point of paper into the long blue stroke of the answer. A section that names problems still opens on what Loom does about them, so the emblem says solved rather than wrong.

=>

const pairsEmblem: Carpet = {
  rows: ['.......', '.......', '.....b.', '....b..', '.a.b...', '..p....', '.......'],
  threads: { '.': '#14204A', p: '#F2EFE6', a: '#FF6A3D', b: '#2E6FF2' },
}

const pairGroups: ReadonlyArray<PairGroup> = [
  {
    title: 'Prose and code that agree',
    pairs: [
      {
        problem: 'Specs and code drift. Sync machinery becomes a system within a system.',
        answer: 'Prose and code stay in sync because they live together and change together.',
      },
      {
        problem:
          'Generating code from a spec is never deterministic. Give an agent the same spec twice and it writes two different programs.',
        answer:
          'In Loom the code is written down, not regenerated. Prose and code are both source, so every change is an edit we can review, never a fresh roll of the dice.',
      },
      {
        problem:
          'In large codebases, prose can no longer be traced to its code, and the same ground gets covered again in new chapters.',
        answer:
          'Every passage sits beside the code it describes, and the table of contents is generated from the chapters themselves.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'A program we can read',
    pairs: [
      {
        problem: 'Natural language does not follow the order a compiler demands.',
        answer:
          'With Loom we write a program in the order we reason about it, and tangling puts each piece where the compiler wants it.',
      },
      {
        problem:
          'A program is a graph of files and modules pointing at one another, with no shape of the whole and no clear place to start reading.',
        answer:
          'A Loom project can grow into a book. Its parts and chapters read top to bottom, so every piece has a place and every reader a place to start.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Tooling we can adopt today',
    pairs: [
      {
        problem:
          'Adopting a new format usually means converting a whole codebase before any of it pays off.',
        answer:
          'Loom can host one file at a time. The rest of the codebase stays as it is until we choose to move it.',
      },
      {
        problem:
          'Literate programming tools were each built for one language, as WEB was for Pascal and CWEB for C, or they treat every language as plain text.',
        answer:
          'Loom composes any language, and one chapter can mix several. Literate programming reaches every language a project is written in.',
      },
      {
        problem:
          'In the editor, a literate document gets no completion, no diagnostics and no go-to-definition, because its tools understand none of the code.',
        answer: 'Loom gives every supported language a real language server.',
      },
    ],
  },
]

type Hue = { readonly ink: string; readonly tint: string }

const hues: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<Hue> = [
  { ink: '#FF6A3D', tint: '#FDEDE6' },
  { ink: '#2E6FF2', tint: '#E9F0FE' },
  { ink: '#00B37E', tint: '#E4F5EF' },
  { ink: '#8B5CF6', tint: '#F0EAFE' },
  { ink: '#E8318A', tint: '#FCE8F1' },
  { ink: '#FFC53D', tint: '#FDF3DF' },
  { ink: '#14204A', tint: '#E8EAF1' },
]

~

The two marks are the bug and the check from the site's set of line icons, so they keep their shape and weight at the small size the cards set them in. A problem here is the kind of bug a developer files and fixes, and the icon is the one their own tools draw for it. Each sits beside its line at the offset that sets it on the line's first baseline.

The icons draw in the current text colour, and the stylesheet gives each its own. The bug wears the `loom eject` red and the check wears Loom's green for paper, so each brightens on the dark page along with everything else.

=>

const problemMark: Html = ih.span([ih.Class('why-bug'), ih.AriaHidden(true)], [bugIcon()])

const answerMark: Html = ih.span([ih.Class('why-a'), ih.AriaHidden(true)], [checkIcon()])

const pairLine = (mark: Html, text: string): Html =>
  ih.div([ih.Class('why-line')], [mark, ih.span([], [text])])

const hueAt = (at: number): Hue =>
  Option.getOrElse(Array.get(hues, at % hues.length), () => Array.headNonEmpty(hues))

const pairCard = (pair: Pair, at: number): Html => {
  const hue = hueAt(at)
  return ih.div(
    [ih.Class('why-pair'), ih.Style({ '--pair-ink': hue.ink, '--pair-tint': hue.tint })],
    [
      pairLine(problemMark, pair.problem),
      pairLine(answerMark, pair.answer),
    ],
  )
}

~

A group draws its title and then its cards, numbering each card from `from`, the count of cards in the groups before it. `pairSet` sets the section head over the groups. It works out each group's starting point with `Array.scan`, which runs a total along the groups, and hands each group its own.

=>

const pairGroup = (group: PairGroup, from: number): Html =>
  ih.div(
    [ih.Class('why-group')],
    [
      ih.h3([ih.Class('why-group-title')], [group.title]),
      ih.div(
        [ih.Class('why-pair-set')],
        Array.map(group.pairs, (pair, at) => pairCard(pair, from + at)),
      ),
    ],
  )

const pairSet = (): Html =>
  ih.div(
    [ih.Class('why-cases')],
    [
      ih.div(
        [ih.Class('section-head')],
        [
          carpetMark(pairsEmblem, '42px', broadSeam),
          ih.div(
            [ih.Class('lines')],
            [ih.span([ih.Class('eyebrow')], ['Problem and solution']), ih.h2([], ['What Loom solves'])],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      ...Array.zipWith(
        pairGroups,
        Array.scan(pairGroups, 0, (from, group) => from + group.pairs.length),
        pairGroup,
      ),
    ],
  )

## The section

The motivation is one section of the landing's kind: a woven mark, an eyebrow and a heading, then the content under them. Its mark is the one the nav already gives this page, so the page and the section that argues for it wear the same carpet. The prose comes first and the pairs after it, since the pairs restate the argument in brief once the reader has read it in full.

Each column of the motivation is a stack of paragraphs. `motivationColumn` draws one.

=>

const motivationColumn = (paragraphs: ReadonlyArray<string>): Html =>
  ih.div(
    [ih.Class('why-column')],
    Array.map(paragraphs, (paragraph) => ih.p([ih.Class('prose')], [paragraph])),
  )

const whyMotivation = (): Html =>
  ih.div(
    [ih.Class('section'), ih.Id('why-pairs')],
    [
      ih.div(
        [ih.Class('section-head')],
        [
          carpetMark(whyLoomCarpet, '42px', broadSeam),
          ih.div(
            [ih.Class('lines')],
            [ih.span([ih.Class('eyebrow')], ['The motivation']), ih.h2([], ['The missing why'])],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      ih.div(
        [ih.Class('why-prose')],
        [motivationColumn(motivation.question), motivationColumn(motivation.answer)],
      ),
      pairSet(),
    ],
  )

export const whyLoom = (): ReadonlyArray<Html> => [whyHead(), whyMotivation()]

## What this module draws on

Effect lends `Array` to map and number the pairs, and `Option` to pick each card's hue. Foldkit lends the inert builder and the `Html` type. The page head lends the head itself. The components lend the section's carpet mark, the carpet the nav already gives this page, and the bug and check icons.

=>

import { Array, Option } from 'effect'
import { type Html, inertHtml as ih } from 'foldkit/html'
import { broadSeam, bugIcon, type Carpet, carpetMark, checkIcon, whyLoomCarpet } from './components'
import { pageHead } from './page-head'

